<div class="min-h-screen text-white">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <nav class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-4 animate-fade-in">
      <a href="/agents-dashboard" class="hover:text-slate-300 transition-colors">&larr; All projects</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="mb-10 animate-fade-in">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight hero-shimmer">Getting Started</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-400">
        Drop a task spec, queue it, watch an opencode agent dispatch it end-to-end.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="task-md text-sm">
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>task-orchestrator</strong> is a task queue plus agent
        dispatcher. You draft a spec in the kanban editor, pick the agent
        + model, and click <strong>Queue → agent</strong>. A global
        dispatcher (Bun, one process for the whole workspace) picks the
        row up via a solidb live-query, spins a fresh git worktree, runs
        <code>/do-task</code> then <code>/review-task</code>, and either
        opens a PR or marks the task done.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every task row lives in solidb. The web UI (kanban, task editor,
        run viewer) drives the same loop — no filesystem watchers, no
        per-repo daemons.
      </p>

      <h2>Setup</h2>
      <p>The dispatcher and the web UI need these on the host:</p>
      <pre><code>gh auth login                                 # gh CLI authenticated against your fork
opencode --version                            # opencode CLI in PATH
bun --version                                 # Bun runtime for the dispatcher
jq --version                                  # JSON stream formatter</code></pre>
      <p>
        Configure solidb credentials, run migrations, then install and
        start the global systemd-user dispatcher:
      </p>
      <pre><code># Configure (one-time)
cp .env.example .env                      # or write SOLIDB_* vars manually
soli db:migrate up                        # create collections

# Install + start the global dispatcher
~/workspace/soli/task-orchestrator/bin/install-dispatcher

# Run the web UI
soli serve . --dev                        # http://localhost:5011</code></pre>
      <p>Inspect the dispatcher:</p>
      <pre><code>systemctl --user status task-dispatch.service
journalctl --user -u task-dispatch.service -f
tail -f ~/.local/state/task-orchestrator/dispatcher.log</code></pre>

      <h2>Daily use</h2>
      <p>The browser flow:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>From <a href="/agents-dashboard">the project list</a>, pick a project. The kanban shows <code>todo / queued / in-progress / review / done</code>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>+ New task</strong>, type a title and a markdown body (or paste rough notes and let <em>Plan with agent</em> structure them).</li>
        <li>Hit <strong>Queue → agent</strong> on the task page. The dispatcher picks the row up within a frame; the kanban swaps it into <code>queued</code> in place via HTMX.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>View run log</strong> to follow the live tail. The page polls every second until the agent settles.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The CLI flow (equivalent, useful for keybindings):</p>
      <pre><code># 1. Draft a task spec
$EDITOR ~/workspace/soli/lang/tasks/todo/SEC-095-foo.md

# 2. Validate + queue (fires the agent)
~/workspace/soli/task-orchestrator/bin/task-queue \
  ~/workspace/soli/lang/tasks/todo/SEC-095-foo.md

# 3. Watch progress
tail -f ~/.local/state/task-orchestrator/lang/SEC-095-foo.log

# 4. See backlog across every repo
~/workspace/soli/task-orchestrator/bin/task-dashboard</code></pre>

      <h2>Configuration</h2>
      <p>Environment variables read by the dispatcher scripts:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Var</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>TASK_ORCH_ROOT</code></td>
            <td><code>~/workspace/soli</code></td>
            <td>Where the dashboard scans for projects with <code>tasks/</code> folders.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>TASK_ORCH_STATE</code></td>
            <td><code>~/.local/state/task-orchestrator</code></td>
            <td>Per-task log + status + PR-URL files written during a run.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>TASK_ORCH_WORKTREES</code></td>
            <td><code>~/.cache/task-orchestrator/worktrees</code></td>
            <td>Where agents check out an isolated copy of the repo. Cleaned up on success.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Failure mode</h2>
      <p>If <code>/review-task</code> rejects the fix or the agent exits non-zero:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The task row is moved to <strong>failed</strong> (out of <code>queued</code> so it can't re-trigger).</li>
        <li>The full log is preserved at <code>~/.local/state/task-orchestrator/&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;task&gt;.log</code>.</li>
        <li>The git worktree is removed; the local branch may still exist for inspection (no PR was opened).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>To retry, edit the task spec from this UI (or move it back to <code>todo</code>) and queue again.</p>

      <h2>State files &amp; worktrees</h2>
      <p>Two paths the dispatcher owns, both safe to inspect or delete between runs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>~/.local/state/task-orchestrator/&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;task&gt;.{log,status,pr}</code> — per-task run artefacts, surfaced live in the run viewer.</li>
        <li><code>~/.cache/task-orchestrator/worktrees/&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;task&gt;/</code> — the git worktree the agent runs in, removed on a successful run.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Stuck on something? Hit <a href="/debug">/debug</a> for the
        live model + state-root snapshot, or read <code>README.md</code>
        in the repo for the source-of-truth reference.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</div>
